    {
      "id": "library/10665-354399/blocks/prose-sec-003",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Resumen ejecutivo",
      "pageStart": 11,
      "pageEnd": 27,
      "target": "../sections/sec-003-resumen-ejecutivo.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Antecedentes\nLa salud digital, o el uso de tecnologías digitales para la salud, se ha convertido en un campo \nde práctica destacado para el empleo de formas rutinarias e innovadoras de tecnologías de la \ninformación y la comunicación (TIC) con el fin de abordar las necesidades de salud. La expresión \n“salud digital” está basada en la cibersalud o eSalud, que se define como “el uso de tecnologías \nde la información y la comunicación en apoyo de la salud y las áreas relacionadas con la salud”. La \n“salud móvil” (mSalud) es un subconjunto de la cibersalud y se define como “el uso de tecnologías",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "The executive summary of the guideline, which defines eHealth, mHealth and digital health and recalls the 2018 World Health Assembly resolution asking WHO for normative guidance on evidence-based digital interventions. It warns that enthusiasm has led to many short-lived implementations with little evaluation of benefits and harms. Using an adapted Tanahashi model, it explains how health system shortfalls limit universal health coverage and how digital interventions might address them. It ends with a figure linking ten recommendations, on birth and death notification, stock notification, telemedicine, targeted client communication, decision support, digital tracking and training, to the health system challenges they address.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
